{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe review gates currently read effective criteria and routing through multiple seams. Code review then consults packaged-only overlay and detector registries, so a project disable or retune can be ignored and an activated project DET criterion can disappear. Separate reads can also describe different repository policy states. RP-06 requires one immutable authority before any selection or model call.\n\n## Approach\nCreate a repository-bound `CriteriaSnapshot` in `rebar.llm.criteria`. It compiles effective built-ins, project LLM criteria, project DET criteria, prompt/routing/posture/applicability metadata, source locations, and one deterministic digest from the provided repository root and already-resolved operation inputs. Consumers receive the snapshot; they do not reread ambient policy.\n\nKeep applicability vocabularies gate-specific. For project code-review LLM criteria, validate `applies_to` as a non-empty list of non-empty repository-relative globs and define `[\"**\"]` as repository-wide. `[\"**\"]` selects the criterion **unconditionally**, including for a review whose changed-files set is empty — this exactly reproduces the prior ungated `applies_to: []` short-circuit (`if not globs: return True`), so repository-wide never regresses to the `any(glob_match …)`-over-empty-`changed_files` = False edge. A scoped glob that matches no changed path produces a typed non-applicable selection reason. Built-in secondary triggers remain unchanged, project DET selection remains detector-owned, and plan review retains `applies_at` and `trigger`.\n\nWhere the new rule lives (do not regress the shared load contract): the existing shared `criteria/overlay.py::_validate_applies_to` deliberately permits an empty list as the \"ungated\" spelling and is called for BOTH gates (ADR 0074); it stays intact so plan-review project criteria (which carry no `applies_to`) and any other gate are unaffected. The stricter \"non-empty globs, `[\"**\"]` = repository-wide, unconditional-select\" rule is applied ONLY to `code_review` `project.`-prefixed LLM criteria, at snapshot-compile in the new `CriteriaSnapshot`/`overlay` code-review-gated path — not in the shared validator. Migrating `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` entry `project.review-phase-boundaries` from `[]` to `[\"**\"]` is precisely what keeps THIS repo's overlay legal under the new code-review rule while leaving its current all-review membership explicit and unchanged.\n\n### Alternatives / prior art\n`CriteriaSnapshot` is a **projection composed with**, not a duplicate of, the repo's existing immutable-authority machinery: ADR 0098's `OperationSnapshot` (an operation resolves one immutable, non-secret snapshot and never rereads ambient config) is the resolution-time authority this snapshot is derived under; ADR 0017 established the one shared `rebar.llm.criteria` delegation layer this story extends (delegation, not rip-and-replace); and `overlay.py::_overlay_signature` already computes a sha256 content digest feeding the per-`(gate, repo, signature)` memoized `_effective_routing_cached` immutable view. The `CriteriaSnapshot` digest **reuses/extends** that existing `_overlay_signature` content digest rather than inventing a parallel one. The reason a single effective authority is preferable to tightening the gate-specific registry reads in place: the current defects (a code-review project disable ignored, an activated project DET dropped, two reads describing different repository states) are exactly the cross-read divergences that survive in-place tightening — one compiled snapshot handed to consumers removes the divergent-read class structurally. The ADR records this comparison.\n\nRecord an ADR describing snapshot ownership, gate-specific applicability, the explicit global-glob spelling, and why a single effective authority is preferable to gate-specific registry reads. The snapshot is data and policy only; it does not interpret YAML/BPMN topology, execute criteria, or decide verdicts.\n\n## Migration and Rollback\nAdd the snapshot alongside existing registry readers. Golden contract tests compare the snapshot-derived selection inputs with current behavior when no project overlay is present and allow only the approved corrections for effective disables/retunes, project DET inclusion, and project LLM applicability. Existing registry APIs remain as compatibility adapters until the dependent cutover stories land; rollback is a code revert, not a new configuration flag.\n\n## Testing\nUse in-process unit and real producer-to-consumer contract tests. One tier lower is insufficient because the known defects occur between the overlay producer and packaged-routing consumers. Tests feed real project routing files through the loader and snapshot compiler, then assert exact selected policy, source provenance, and digest behavior. Negative controls cover built-in empty secondary triggers and plan-review routing, which must not adopt the new project-LLM glob rule. One contract case exercises the diverging empty-`changed_files` edge and asserts a `[\"**\"]` code-review project criterion still selects (proving the migration is behavior-preserving there).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A real project overlay compiled through `CriteriaSnapshot` yields one immutable digest-bound view containing effective built-in, project LLM, and project DET policy with source provenance.\n- [ ] A project code-review LLM criterion with an empty, missing, non-list, or blank `applies_to` fails before any model call with its criterion id, configuration location, and the remedy `use [\"**\"] for repository-wide`.\n- [ ] Given root, nested, and dot changed paths, `applies_to: [\"**\"]` selects the criterion exactly once.\n- [ ] `applies_to: [\"**\"]` selects the criterion unconditionally when `changed_files` is empty (the diverging edge), and a contract test asserts this empty-changed-files case selects, proving the `[]`→`[\"**\"]` migration preserves the prior ungated behavior.\n- [ ] A nonmatching scoped glob records a typed non-applicable selection reason without executing the criterion.\n- [ ] `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` declares `project.review-phase-boundaries.applies_to` as `[\"**\"]`.\n- [ ] A contract test proves `project.review-phase-boundaries` review membership is unchanged by the `[]`→`[\"**\"]` migration, including the empty-changed-files case above.\n- [ ] The shared `_validate_applies_to` empty-list-legal contract is left intact for plan-review project criteria (a negative-control test proves plan-review routing does not adopt the code-review non-empty-glob rule).\n- [ ] Contract tests prove effective built-in disables/retunes are present in the snapshot.\n- [ ] Contract tests prove activated project DET entries are present in the snapshot.\n- [ ] A negative-control test proves built-in secondary-trigger semantics remain unchanged in the snapshot.\n- [ ] An ADR documents snapshot ownership, digest inputs (its reuse of the existing `_overlay_signature` content digest), gate-specific applicability, the prior-art comparison against ADR 0098/0017, compatibility adapters, and the revert-based rollback."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787336252295557001,"uuid":"2aefa8e8-d1b8-4703-af3e-2d879596a56d"}